WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:04.720
Speaker 1: Hey, David, what would happen if
you abolished the minimum wage?

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.300
OK, so this there's been a lot of talk about
a 15 dollar minimum wage lately.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:15.500
And we learned last week that Chipotle, in
order to account for paying 15 bucks an hour,

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.220
is raising prices four percent, which is a
really, really modest increase.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:24.760
It's like 26 cents on your average chicken
burrito, even though everybody will earn fifteen

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:25.980
dollars an hour.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:30.970
And there are some sort of libertarian right
wing types who say, listen, the minimum wage

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:38.550
is an artificial price floor and it is simply
a bad it's a price control.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:40.500
It's bad for the economy.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:44.550
It keeps people out of the workforce if you
eliminated the minimum wage.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:45.550
Sure.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:50.470
Wages might come down a little bit, but way
more people would end up becoming employed.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:54.420
And that would be good for the economy because
then they would pay taxes and little.

00:00:54.420 --> 00:01:01.930
OK, first of all, if you saw a decrease to
the minimum wage, that's significant and it

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:03.359
led to more employment.

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:08.730
A lot of those people, because the wage would
be so low, would would not or would barely

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.390
pay any federal taxes.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:16.140
So in reality, the tax thing may even be be
wrong, number one.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.160
Number two, we have a counterexample to this.

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.790
OK, the value of the minimum wage.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:27.970
Remember, if you have a seven dollar minimum
wage and you have inflation, the value of

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:32.770
that minimum wage actually goes down if you're
the employer.

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:37.870
And there is inflation, meaning your product
or service sells for more, but you pay the

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:43.300
same nominal minimum wage, you are actually
seeing a decrease to the minimum wage in real

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.580
terms.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:51.330
We had a situation in the 1980s where the
minimum wage, the actual value of the minimum

00:01:51.330 --> 00:01:54.610
wage went down about a third because of inflation.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:59.530
And we actually saw teen employment drop site
slightly.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:02.030
This is the big claim from the right.

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:07.560
All of these teenagers and entry level workers
and what we call less skilled workers, they

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:11.150
would have way more employment if the minimum
wage were to go down.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:16.019
Well, the minimum wage did go down in real
terms during the 1980s and significantly so

00:02:16.019 --> 00:02:17.989
just under a third, 32 percent.

00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:19.689
I believe the number is.

00:02:19.689 --> 00:02:24.810
And you did not see teen employment explode
because it all of a sudden became really cheap

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:25.810
to hire people.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:29.999
It got cheaper for businesses to hire because
of that stagnant minimum wage.

00:02:29.999 --> 00:02:31.099
And they didn't.

00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:35.629
And as I've said so many times before, when
it comes to taxation and hiring and the minimum

00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:42.629
wage, if a business is understaffed relative
to the demand for their product or service,

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:45.999
then it would make sense to go and add a new
worker.

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:51.879
And if you can get that worker for less, well,
even better for the business, I guess.

00:02:51.879 --> 00:02:56.980
But it's not profitable to just add workers
because it's cheaper to add them.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:58.159
It doesn't make sense.

00:02:58.159 --> 00:02:59.159
Think about it.

00:02:59.159 --> 00:03:05.660
You know, with The David Pakman Show, if we
actually have a an unmet need, either because

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:12.239
we have so much demand for advertising that
I need more salespeople or we have so much

00:03:12.239 --> 00:03:17.579
demand for memberships that I need to bring
on a full time web developer to keep up with

00:03:17.579 --> 00:03:20.590
the website traffic or whatever the case may
be.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:25.209
Just because it becomes cheaper to hire someone
doesn't mean I'm actually going to go and

00:03:25.209 --> 00:03:29.219
hire someone unless I'm servicing demand.

00:03:29.219 --> 00:03:32.829
Now, what would actually increase demand?

00:03:32.829 --> 00:03:37.810
And this is where the right just kind of seems
to not not get it or pretend not to get it.

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:40.639
What would increase demand for membership's?

00:03:40.639 --> 00:03:43.159
It would be people having more money.

00:03:43.159 --> 00:03:49.060
And one way to put more money in the pockets
of people who might only borderline be able

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:52.549
to afford a membership right now would be
a minimum wage increase.

00:03:52.549 --> 00:03:58.239
Now, I think there are some fair assessments
about the minimum wage that come from the

00:03:58.239 --> 00:03:59.239
right.

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:04.960
One is most people don't make minimum wage,
and because wages tend to be sticky, wages

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:06.800
don't tend to come down.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.370
If you got rid of the minimum wage, it's unlikely
that most people's wages would come down.

00:04:12.370 --> 00:04:16.340
If you're in a job where you make the equivalent
of 25 bucks an hour, it might be twenty five

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:19.759
bucks an hour or it might just be a fifty
thousand dollar a year salary.

00:04:19.759 --> 00:04:20.780
Right.

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:26.240
If the minimum wage went down or even was
eliminated, it's not hugely likely, at least

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.280
right away, that you would end up earning
less money.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.229
But there is a particularly vulnerable class
of workers, particularly when we're talking

00:04:35.229 --> 00:04:42.419
about immigrants, talking about people in
disadvantaged neighborhoods that already often

00:04:42.419 --> 00:04:46.830
have the sort of structural disadvantages
of terrible public transit and so on and so

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:47.830
forth.

00:04:47.830 --> 00:04:51.040
Those are the first folks who are going to
be taken advantage of.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.979
And there's going to be this downward pressure.

00:04:53.979 --> 00:05:01.129
And the whole point of the minimum wage is
not to insure everybody makes 50 grand a year.

00:05:01.129 --> 00:05:05.479
That would require a twenty five dollar minimum
wage at every job in many states that are

00:05:05.479 --> 00:05:06.659
low cost of living it.

00:05:06.659 --> 00:05:09.050
That's not a it's just not an equilibrium
wage.

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:11.720
And I know there's many people who say the
minimum wage should be twenty five bucks an

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:12.720
hour.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.639
You know, in rural Alabama, a twenty five
dollar an hour minimum wage is out of equilibrium

00:05:17.639 --> 00:05:21.509
with business, profitability and cost of living
in some big cities.

00:05:21.509 --> 00:05:23.180
That probably is the right minimum wage.

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:29.271
But the point here is you are going to see
the most disadvantaged and those with the

00:05:29.271 --> 00:05:34.530
least market power to advocate for themselves
taking advantage of now, the right will then

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:39.740
come back in and say, well, they can then
organize and they can all agree we're not

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:44.460
going to work for less than X, and they can
push prices up and demand to be paid more.

00:05:44.460 --> 00:05:45.830
That's what unions do now.

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:49.479
And those same right wingers are against the
unions and they're against collective bargaining

00:05:49.479 --> 00:05:50.910
and on and on and on.

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:55.139
So you really have to step back from the talking
points on this.

00:05:55.139 --> 00:05:59.160
If you eliminate the minimum wage, is it likely
that a lot of people would start working for

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.710
50 cents an hour?

00:06:00.710 --> 00:06:02.629
No, that's not likely.

00:06:02.629 --> 00:06:07.189
But the federal minimum wage is already pathetic.

00:06:07.189 --> 00:06:11.870
And what the other thing, by the way, that's
important to remember is reducing the minimum

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:19.199
wage will shift the burden of paying people
away from businesses and towards the government.

00:06:19.199 --> 00:06:24.050
If you reduce the minimum wage, you're also
basically setting up a bigger government handout

00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:29.990
and subsidy because the benefits and welfare
programs that individuals will start to qualify

00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:31.669
for if you scrap the minimum.

00:06:31.669 --> 00:06:35.880
Wage and you see somewhat of a reduction,
you know, seven, 25, maybe some people start

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:41.600
working for six or five dollars an hour for
fifty or whatever, the the fact that more

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.330
people will work, which doesn't appear to
be true.

00:06:44.330 --> 00:06:46.639
But even if it were true, more people will
work.

00:06:46.639 --> 00:06:54.129
If you reduce the minimum wage, they whatever
they pay in to the government, you will lose

00:06:54.129 --> 00:07:01.080
because they will qualify for government welfare
programs that cost the government money that

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:02.650
come out of taxes anyway.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:07.139
So the point of the minimum wage is let's
agree and establish some minimum that makes

00:07:07.139 --> 00:07:15.030
sense based on cost of living, based on equilibrium
wages, based on what we consider to to be

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:16.830
a reasonable standard to set.

00:07:16.830 --> 00:07:19.729
We're not talking about a fifty dollar an
hour, one hundred dollars an hour minimum

00:07:19.729 --> 00:07:26.370
wage, and it should be in balance with the
broader economy and prevent the most vulnerable

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.120
from being taken advantage of.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:29.449
That's really the point.

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:34.110
And the hypotheses about, well, if more money
is good, pay people 100 bucks, or if you get

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:37.290
rid of the minimum wage, people will be working
for 10 cents an hour.

00:07:37.290 --> 00:07:53.560
You're focusing on the the extremes and you're
really sort 

00:07:53.560 --> 00:08:50.950
of missing the big picture

